#import "../../template.typ": *

#plant-header("Autumn Joy Sedum", "Sedum spectabile 'Autumn Joy'", "sedum")[
  Autumn Joy Sedum is one of the most reliable four-season perennials for the Front Range. Pink flowers in August–September mature to rust-bronze and persist through winter. Succulent, drought-adapted foliage. Very low maintenance. Works in any well-drained spot. Established 2+ years — on drip irrigation.
]

=== Watering

Established plants on drip: in clay soil, target 1–2 gallons per watering event, once per week in summer. For 1 gph emitters, run 15–20 minutes. The clay holds moisture significantly longer than average — check soil moisture at 3-inch depth before each cycle and skip irrigation if still damp. Water less frequently than you think necessary: once every 10–14 days is often adequate in summer. Reduce to once every 2–3 weeks in spring and fall. Dry conditions improve plant quality and reduce stem flopping.

=== Fertilization

Performs best with minimal fertilization — lean conditions produce compact, self-supporting plants. Do not apply phosphorus (your soil P is HIGH). If anything, a very light application of ammonium sulfate or potassium sulfate in early spring is acceptable. No fall fertilization.

=== Pruning & Maintenance

Leave the rust-bronze seed heads standing through winter — they are one of the great winter ornamentals and provide seed for birds. Cut back to the ground in late winter or early spring as new rosettes emerge at the base. Avoid cutting the new growth. If plants become floppy in summer (a sign of too much nitrogen or shade), cut all stems back by one-third in June to force a more compact rebloom.

=== Wildlife Value

Autumn Joy Sedum provides modest but consistent wildlife value. The flower heads attract native bees, bumble bees, and cabbage white butterflies through August and September. The persistent dry seed heads are important for house finches, which pick the seeds throughout fall and winter. American robins may visit as well. The succulent foliage is generally unappealing to cottontail rabbits, though they occasionally nibble new spring growth on established plants. Northern flickers and red-wing blackbirds occasionally forage at the base.

=== Pests & Disease

Sedum has excellent pest resistance. #link("https://www.google.com/search?q=sedum+crown+rot+treatment")[Crown rot] in wet clay is the primary risk — addressed through careful watering management in your clay soil (see Watering). Occasional #link("https://www.google.com/search?q=sedum+aphids+treatment")[aphids] on new spring growth — treat with insecticidal soap or a water spray. In dry Colorado conditions, fungal diseases are uncommon. Over-fertilization with nitrogen is a more common cause of plant problems (flopping) than disease.

=== Other Care

One of the most foolproof perennials in this collection. Divide clumps every 3–4 years in spring when the center begins to die out. Do not plant where water pools in winter. Keep mulch away from the crown to ensure air circulation.
